  "query_preview": "..\nThe system that brought precipitation to the Inland Northwest \nyesterday into this (Tuesday) morning has moved out of the area, \nallowing for a short break in precipitation through this afternoon. \nA second, stronger upper-level low will move in later tonight, bringing \na renewed chance of showers to lower elevations, slightly breezy winds, \nand mountain snow accumulations. As this colder system moves in late this \nevening, snow levels will drop to around 2000 to 3000 feet. Mountain \nsnow will start around 5pm this afternoon in the Cascades and in the \nfar northern ID Panhandle. The greatest snow accumulations are expected \nat Stevens and Washington Passes. Probabilities for 6 inches of snow \nthrough Wednesday afternoon are 70% at Stevens Pass and 45% at Washington \nPass. Other passes, such as Sherman and Lookout Passes, are set to see \naround 2-4 inches of snow. Stevens and Washington Passes will see winter \ndriving conditions, and a Winter Weather Advisory has been issued for these \ntwo passes from 5pm tonight through 5pm tomorrow (Wednesday). Additionally, \nsubfreezing temperatures will present a threat for outdoor enthusiasts. \nIf you are planning to be in these areas over the next few days, please take \nprecautions. \n\nWhile winter impacts along mountain passes will be the most impactful part of \nthis system, there is a chance of showers across elevations below 3000 feet \nalongside wind gusts in the basin areas. Winds will increase from the southwest \nas the low moves inland, occurring tonight (Tuesday) through tomorrow morning. \nWind gusts in the Columbia Basin, Spokane, Palouse, and West Plains could reach \n25-30 mph. While these breezy conditions are expected, widespread wind related \nimpacts are not anticipated. Rain is expected across many lower elevations, with \nthe highest totals expected in the Idaho Panhandle and northern mountains, which \ncould see up to an inch of rain. Temperatures cool after the system moves through, \nwith highs in the 40s and low 50s and lows in the 30s.\n\nFriday through Monday: The low is expected to remain just off the coast of \nWashington on Friday and Saturday while gradually weakening. Precipitation chances \nwill decrease during this period, with any remaining showers confined to over the mountains \ndue to orographic lift. Although snow levels could fall to around 2,000 feet across \nnorth-central Washington, limited precipitation chances are expected to keep additional \nsnow accumulations minimal. By Sunday into Monday, the low is forecast to shift south as \na ridge builds into the region, supporting a gradual warming trend into Monday. Ensemble \nclusters show approximately 80% support for this pattern. /AS",
